import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Handl Ecosystem","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"ecosystem/index.md","filePath":"ecosystem/index.md"}`),a={name:`ecosystem/index.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="handl-ecosystem" tabindex="-1">Handl Ecosystem <a class="header-anchor" href="#handl-ecosystem" aria-label="Permalink to “Handl Ecosystem”">​</a></h1><p>Handl is a modular language infrastructure built around a categorical, runtime-independent IR. Its foundation is a closed arrow calculus with globular higher-categorical structure, allowing computation, laws, and transformations to share a common representation.</p><p>The ecosystem separates this foundation from the engines that process it, the abstractions built on top of it, and the environments in which it operates.</p><h2 id="_1-kernel" tabindex="-1">1. Kernel <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-kernel" aria-label="Permalink to “1. Kernel”">​</a></h2><p>The Kernel defines Handl&#39;s computational foundation: its arrow calculus, IR, value representation, and language schema.</p><p>The calculus is based on LWY (Lindley–Wadler–Yallop) and Sanada&#39;s arrow calculus, extended with graded effects and higher-categorical structure.</p><p>Key properties:</p><ul><li><strong>Closed arrow calculus</strong>: computation is expressed through six fundamental combinators (<code>arr</code>, <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>, <code>first</code>, <code>choice</code>, <code>loop</code>, <code>app</code>), rather than lambda calculus.</li><li><strong>Globular structure</strong>: the IR represents morphisms across dimensions. Programs are 1-morphisms, laws and rewrites are 2-morphisms, and higher transformations extend this structure.</li><li><strong>Composition and coherence</strong>: dimension-indexed composition, boundaries, rewriting rules, and coherence relationships allow higher-dimensional structures to compose consistently.</li><li><strong>Graded effects</strong>: based on the Graded Modal Sanada Arrow Calculus, using graded operations and handlers to represent and interpret effects.</li><li><strong>Language as data</strong>: inspired by Cimini&#39;s work, languages are described declaratively through syntax, grammar, AST shapes, evaluation schemes, type rules, and transformations, rather than being hardcoded into engines.</li><li><strong>Serializable computation</strong>: arrows are represented as data, enabling inspection, transformation, serialization, and interpretation by different engines.</li></ul><p>The Kernel provides a stable foundation without prescribing a particular runtime or execution strategy.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/kernel" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">github.com/handl-lang/kernel</a> · <a href="/docs/kernel/">docs</a></p><h2 id="_2-engines" tabindex="-1">2. Engines <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-engines" aria-label="Permalink to “2. Engines”">​</a></h2><p>Independent components that process Handl representations. Engines can analyze, transform, compile, or execute the IR.</p><ul><li><strong>Compilation</strong>: LLVM (native), Triton, CUDA — <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/compile-llvm" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>compile-llvm</code></a>, <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/compile-triton" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>compile-triton</code></a>, <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/compile-cuda" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>compile-cuda</code></a>.</li><li><strong>Execution</strong>: Cranelift, bytecode, tree-walk interpreter — <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/engine-exec-cranelift" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>engine-exec-cranelift</code></a>, <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/engine-exec-bytecode" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>engine-exec-bytecode</code></a>, <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/engine-exec-tree-walk" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>engine-exec-tree-walk</code></a>.</li><li><strong>Transformation</strong>: IR-to-IR rewriting, desugaring, optimization — <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/engine-transform-rewrite" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>engine-transform-rewrite</code></a>.</li><li><strong>Language processing</strong>: lexer, parser, typechecker, linter, formatter — <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/engine-lex-pattern" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>engine-lex-pattern</code></a>, <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/engine-parse-pratt" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>engine-parse-pratt</code></a>, <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/engine-typecheck" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>engine-typecheck</code></a>, <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/engine-lint" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>engine-lint</code></a>, <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/engine-fmt" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>engine-fmt</code></a>.</li></ul><p>Engines are interchangeable consumers and processors of the shared representation, not intrinsic parts of the Kernel.</p><h2 id="_3-libraries-lib" tabindex="-1">3. Libraries (<code>lib-*</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-libraries-lib" aria-label="Permalink to “3. Libraries (lib-*)”">​</a></h2><p>Reusable Handl libraries (<code>.hnl</code>) providing foundational and domain-specific functionality, such as mathematics, strings, logic, and other utilities.</p><p>Libraries provide runtime-agnostic abstractions: the same APIs can be used across different execution environments, with runtime-specific implementations preserving compatible interfaces and semantics.</p><p>This allows application code and higher-level libraries to remain independent of the underlying runtime.</p><h2 id="_4-kits-kit" tabindex="-1">4. Kits (<code>kit-*</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-kits-kit" aria-label="Permalink to “4. Kits (kit-*)”">​</a></h2><p>Curated collections of libraries and components targeting particular environments or use cases — testing, server-side development, and web services, for example.</p><p>Kits provide ready-to-use environments by assembling compatible libraries, engines, and supporting components.</p><h2 id="_5-tools" tabindex="-1">5. Tools <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-tools" aria-label="Permalink to “5. Tools”">​</a></h2><p>Developer utilities and generators that facilitate development and interoperability with external systems.</p><p>SDK generators produce type-safe Handl APIs from existing interfaces:</p><ul><li><a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/sdkgen-python" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>sdkgen-python</code></a> — Python interoperability.</li><li><a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/sdkgen-c" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>sdkgen-c</code></a> — C interoperability, including libc, libcurl, and zlib.</li><li><a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/sdkgen-webidl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>sdkgen-webidl</code></a> — Web APIs, including Fetch and Console.</li><li><a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/sdkgen-cli" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>sdkgen-cli</code></a> — type-safe APIs for spawning and interacting with CLI processes, providing SDK-like interfaces for tools such as Git.</li></ul><h2 id="_6-host-extensions" tabindex="-1">6. Host &amp; Extensions <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-host-extensions" aria-label="Permalink to “6. Host &amp; Extensions”">​</a></h2><p>The distribution and integration layer exposing Handl to users and development environments.</p><ul><li><strong>Host</strong>: CLI and LSP — <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/host-cli" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>host-cli</code></a>, <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/host-lsp" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>host-lsp</code></a>.</li><li><strong>Extensions</strong>: VS Code and Neovim integrations — <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/extension-vscode" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>extension-vscode</code></a>, <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/extension-neovim" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>extension-neovim</code></a>.</li></ul><h2 id="_7-documentation" tabindex="-1">7. Documentation <a class="header-anchor" href="#_7-documentation" aria-label="Permalink to “7. Documentation”">​</a></h2><p>Aggregated documentation across the ecosystem.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/docs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">github.com/handl-lang/docs</a></p><h2 id="_8-products-research" tabindex="-1">8. Products &amp; Research <a class="header-anchor" href="#_8-products-research" aria-label="Permalink to “8. Products &amp; Research”">​</a></h2><p>Domain-specific applications and research projects built on Handl.</p><ul><li><strong>MLOpt</strong> — optimization of existing machine learning models for embedded architectures. <a href="https://github.com/handl-lang/mlopt" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">github.com/handl-lang/mlopt</a></li></ul><hr><p><strong>Architectural principle:</strong> The Kernel defines a compositional, higher-categorical computational model. Engines interpret and transform its representations. Libraries provide runtime-agnostic abstractions, while Kits assemble them into usable environments. Tools bridge Handl with external systems, and Hosts expose the ecosystem to users and development environments.</p><ul><li><a href="/docs/ecosystem/bibliography.html">Bibliography</a> — the academic foundations.</li></ul>`,37)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};